* About IHHT/IHT

IHT/IHHT is a Method applying interchangably low and high Oxygen content.

The body reacts to hypoxic stimuly in several stages which can be categorized
in Short mid and long term adaptions. They include effects on

- Cardiac Output
- Vasodynamics
- Breathing and Ventiation
- Regulation of Inflammation
- Metabolism
- Mitochondrial Quality
  
  The underlying Molecular Mechanisms may be linked to Mitochondrial adaption (Biogenisis/Mitoptosis)
  and epigenetic changes through HIF. However the Evidence on these underlying mechanisms are scarce
  especially for IHT/IHHT

Commercial advertisements for IHHT/IHT devices often portray the treatment as "Cell Training,"
"Hypoxic Training," or "Altitude Training," often referencing mitochondrial/cellular adaptations, Hypoxia
Inducible Factor, and the Nobel Prize in Medicine. However, the methodologies and research fields cited
typically pertain to fundamental physiological and pathophysiological principles (Nobel Prize in Medicine 2019)
or studies from altitude research where hypoxia is applied under different conditions for longer durations
alongside exercise. While these studies may guide further research and development of IHHT/IHT, they often
do not directly apply to disease treatment or clinical applications of IHHT/IHT.

